course: world
lesson: 180
title: Correlation is not cause: ice cream and sunburn
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: When two things go up and down together, grown-ups say they are correlated, but that doesn't mean one causes the other. On hot days more ice creams are sold and more people get sunburnt, yet ice cream doesn't burn anyone: the sun does both.
explain: So look for a hidden third cause. Older children have bigger feet AND read better, because they are older. Rich countries eat more chocolate AND do more science, because they are rich.
explain: Then check which way the arrow points: big fires get more firefighters and do more damage; hospitals are full of ill people because ill people go there to get better.
explain: And check what you forget: Dad remembers the rainy days after washing the car and forgets all the sunny ones.
explain: Leo's mum says it best: "So if I eat a Magnum in December I'll burn? Love, it's the sun. The sun's doing both."
fact: When two things happen together, one does not have to cause the other; grown-ups say "correlation is not causation".
fact: Hot sunny weather causes both more ice-cream sales and more sunburn.
fact: Older children have bigger feet and have also had more years of reading practice; feet don't help reading.
fact: Big fires get more firefighters and also do more damage, while the firefighters reduce the damage; mixing up which way the cause goes is called reverse causation.
fact: Ill people go to hospital to get better, and hospitals help millions of people every year.
fact: Breakfast probably helps you concentrate, but families who make breakfast may differ in other ways too, so careful tests compare similar families.
fact: Smoking causes lung disease; a lighter is only a sign that someone might smoke.
fact: Washing a car can't change the weather; remembering only the times that match what you believe is called confirmation bias.
fact: A real 2012 article in a medical journal pointed out that countries eating more chocolate win more Nobel Prizes (the most famous prizes for science), partly as a joke; wealth explains both.
fact: Height depends mostly on the genes you get from your family, and also on good food and health while growing up.
fact: More people drown in summer because more people swim in hot weather; swimming where there are lifeguards matters far more than what you eat.
fact: Words people use: "sick" (young people) means great; "ace" means do really well at; "it's giving" means "it looks like"; "no cap" means honestly; "a Magnum" is an ice cream on a stick; "dreich" (Scotland) means grey, wet weather; "motor" (UK) means car; "togs" (Australia) means swimming costume; "heaps" (Australia, NZ) means lots.
exercise: On hot days more ice cream is sold and more people get sunburnt. Leo: "Ice cream causes sunburn!" What causes both? | Hot sunny weather | The ice cream | The sunburn | The ice-cream vans
exercise: Children with bigger feet are better at reading. What is the hidden cause? | Age: older kids have bigger feet and more reading practice | Big feet send more blood up to the brain | Reading books makes children's feet grow | Shoe shops give out free books
exercise: More firefighters at a fire means more damage. Toby: "Firefighters make fires worse!" What is really happening? | Bigger fires get more firefighters and do more damage | Firefighters spread the flames around with their strong hoses | Small fires always get the most firefighters | Damage makes fires grow on its own
exercise: What do grown-ups call it when the cause is the wrong way round? | Reverse causation | A straw man | A red herring | Small print
exercise: Gran notices people in hospital are very ill. "Hospitals make you ill, I'm not going." What is the truth? | Ill people go to hospital to get better | Hospitals make healthy people ill | People in hospital were never ill before | Hospitals are only for visitors
exercise: Children who eat breakfast do better at school. Ruby: "Maybe families who make breakfast also help with homework." What is Ruby pointing out? | There might be other differences between the families | Breakfast can't affect how well children do at school at all | Homework makes children hungry | Only breakfast matters for school
exercise: How could scientists check whether breakfast itself helps? | Compare similar families in careful tests | Ask one child what they ate for breakfast today | Count the cereal boxes sold in the local shops | See which children wake up earliest in the morning
exercise: People who carry lighters are more likely to get lung disease. What really causes the disease? | Smoking | The lighter's metal | The colour of the flame | Carrying things in pockets
exercise: Dad: "Every time I wash the car, it rains." Why does he believe it? | He remembers the rainy days and forgets the sunny ones | Car shampoo makes little rain clouds form right above the car | Clean cars attract rain from the sky | It really rains every time he washes it
exercise: What is remembering only the times that match your belief called? | Confirmation bias | Rosy retrospection | The fence-post error | A slippery slope
exercise: A news report: "Countries that eat more chocolate win more Nobel Prizes!" What explains both? | Wealth: rich countries buy more chocolate and pay for more science | Chocolate makes scientists cleverer | Nobel Prizes are made of chocolate | Prize winners are paid in chocolate
exercise: Harry's grandad drank milk every night and is tall, and his own dad was tall too. What mostly decides height? | Genes, plus good food and health while growing | Only the glass of milk you drink every single night | Only how many hours you sleep | Only the shoes you wear as a child
exercise: In summer more people drown and more ice cream is sold. "Is ice cream dangerous?" What is the hidden cause? | Hot weather: more swimming and more ice cream | Ice cream makes swimmers sink | Swimmers eat ice cream in the water and get cramp | Drowning makes people buy ice cream
exercise: When two things rise together, which questions should you ask? | Is there a third cause, and which way does the arrow go? | Which of the two is more delicious? | Which of the two was shown on TV first, and on what channel? | How loud are the people who said it?
exercise: Which statement is true? | Things can go together without one causing the other | If two things go together, one always causes the other | Things that go together are never connected | Only scientists can ever spot patterns
test: Mia's brother: "So if I wear clown shoes I'll ace my reading test? Sick, I'm buying size 20." Why is it funny? | Feet don't help reading; age causes both | Bigger shoes really do make you better at reading | Size 20 shoes are only ever sold to real clowns | His reading test is mostly about clowns and shoes
test: What would happen if a town sent fewer firefighters to big fires? | The fires would probably do more damage | The fires would shrink straight away on their own | There would be much less damage, with fewer hoses | Nothing would change at all, the damage stays the same
test: Gran: "I'm not going in that hospital, it's full of ill folk." Grandson: "Gran, that's like saying umbrellas cause rain." Why is his comparison good? | Umbrellas appear because of rain, as ill people appear in hospitals | Umbrellas really do cause the rain | Hospitals sell umbrellas to ill people | Rain makes people go to hospital
test: Dad in Scotland on a dreich day: "Washed the motor again, and look, rain. I'm cursed." What is true? | It rains after some washes and not others, like any day | Washing a car really does cause rain, so he should stop doing it | Dad is cursed by the weather | Rain only falls on clean cars
test: In a cartoon a scientist eats a giant chocolate bar and a Nobel medal drops from the sky. Put the cartoon right. | Rich countries have more chocolate and more labs; chocolate doesn't win prizes | Eating chocolate is how scientists win prizes | Medals really fall when you eat chocolate | Nobel Prizes are only given to chocolate makers
test: Kai: "Lighters make you ill, no cap." His aunt: "A lighter's just a lighter, Kai." Who is right? | His aunt; smoking is the cause | Kai; the gas in the lighter causes it | Both; lighters and smoking are equal | Neither; lung disease has no cause
test: Harry: "Did your dad drink milk as a baby giraffe, Grandad?" What is he poking fun at? | The idea that milk alone made the family tall | The idea that baby giraffes drink milk every night | His grandad being very tall | Milk being good food
test: Aussie lifeguard: "It's hot, heaps of people swimming. Grab your togs, but the ice cream's not the danger, mate." What danger does he mean? | Swimming in deep water; swim where lifeguards are | Eating ice cream in hot weather, which gives you cramp | Wearing togs in the sea | The sun melting the ice cream
test: Mum: "Eat your breakfast, it makes you clever." Ruby: "I'm eating it. I'm not suddenly doing calculus." Which is fairest? | Breakfast probably helps you concentrate, but it isn't the whole story | Breakfast turns every child into a genius | Breakfast has no effect on anyone at all | Calculus is only for children who skip breakfast
test: Leo: "Ice cream causes sunburn, it's giving science." What is the better science? | Look for a third thing, the sun, that causes both | Stop eating ice cream altogether to avoid getting sunburn | Eat ice cream only in winter to stay safe | Sunburn is what makes people want ice cream
test: A headline: "Kids who own more books get better marks!" What should you ask first? | What else is different about those families? | Which of the books have the nicest, shiniest covers? | How heavy are all the books if you weigh them? | Are the books brand new or bought second-hand?
test: Your friend notices that whenever the school bell rings, everyone stands up. "The bell pushes us up!" What is the better explanation? | The bell is a signal that it's time to go, so people choose to stand | The bell's sound waves lift children out of chairs | Standing up makes the bell ring | Bells and standing happen together by pure luck
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the simultaneous increase of ice-cream sales and of sunburn does not demonstrate that the former causes the latter. | Both are caused by a third factor, namely hot and sunny weather | Ice cream invariably protects the skin of those who consume it from sunburn | Sunburn is the cause of ice-cream consumption | The two quantities never increase together
exam: It is given that larger fires are attended by a greater number of firefighters and also occasion greater damage. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | The size of the fire determines both quantities, and firefighters reduce the damage | Firefighters are the principal cause of the damage | The damage summons the firefighters to the fire | The number of firefighters has no relation to the fire
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the inference that hospitals cause illness because their occupants are frequently ill? | It inverts cause and effect, since ill persons attend hospitals | It is a sound inference from the observation of hospital patients | It is an instance of the fence-post error | It is consistent with the established facts
exam: Ascertain the factor which principally explains the association between national chocolate consumption and the number of Nobel Prizes awarded. | The wealth of the countries concerned | The nutritional properties of chocolate | The judges' preference for chocolate | The colour of the prize medal
